ge her hair  clothing and body the company behind  pulip also frequently releases limited  edition dolls it has even created a  Sailor Moon doll as well as other  characters from the anime culture queens  of Africa dolls from Nigeria in an  attempt to bring awareness to the  diversity of the original Barbie dolls  Nigerian entrepreneur Tawfik Akua  created a new line of dolls back in 2007  these dolls highlight the diversity of  skin tones and hair texture all while  rocking contemporary African fashion  they are called queens of Africa  it only reconfirmed the need for diverse  dolls when the queens of Africa outsold  the original Barbie dolls in 2014  Vic was inspired to create these dolls  after his own daughter expressed her  desire to have the same skin tone as the  original Barbie he felt the need to  educate young girls about the importance  of being yourself regardless of where  you come from the Queen's of Africa's  motto is to empower the African girl  child they're modeled after three main  African tribes and each Queen has a  different symbol love peace and  endurance and he's not stopping just a  dolls his team is also creating  educational books that teach children  self-confidence and cultural pride they  are also aiming to expand the doll line  and include at least one doll for each  African country along with creating  limited edition dolls to raise funds for  an African education charity  Lottie dolls from Ireland Lottie dolls  are Island's alternative to the famous  Barbie doll with an aim to empower  children to be themselves the company  behind Lottie dolls created dolls that  look like real nine-year-old children  these dog characters are completely  relatable and age-appropriate they  represent different facets of childhood  without any limitation a lot a dog comes  with a diverse range of skin hair and  clothing the world they live in is  called the Banksy Island which is  loosely based on UK's Brown Sea Island  which is the birthplace of man